            <ArticleTitle>The association of hyperuricemia and metabolic syndrome in Thai postmenopausal women.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>552-557</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/13697137.2017.1369513</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to evaluate the association of hyperuricemia and metabolic syndrome (MS) in Thai postmenopausal women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A total of 740 consecutive Thai postmenopausal women were reviewed who were not receiving uric acid or lipid-lowering agents at the Menopause Clinic, Faculty of Medicine Siriraj Hospital, Bangkok, from January 2004 to January 2016. Hyperuricemia was defined as serum uric acid level &gt;5.7 mg/dl. The revised National Cholesterol Education Program (rNCEP) 2004 criteria were used for diagnosis of MS. The association of hyperuricemia and MS was evaluated.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Among 740 women, 162 patients (21.9%) were hyperuricemic. The prevalence of MS was 43.8% and 21.8% for hyperuricemic and normo-uricemic women, respectively. Hyperuricemia was significantly associated with increased risk of MS (odds ratio 2.79, 95% confidence interval 1.93-4.0; p &lt; 0.001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">There was a significant association of hyperuricemia and MS in Thai postmenopausal women. These two co-morbidities were common after menopause. When hyperuricemia is diagnosed, MS should be screened, prevented or treated to decrease cardiovascular disease in the future.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
